<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Bangladesh</h1><p class="st-meta">1971 CE – present · capital Dhaka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The densely populated Bengali nation of the Ganges delta, born in 1971 from a war of liberation against Pakistan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a> became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-pakistan">Dominion of Pakistan</a> in 1947 and was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> in 1955. Grievances over language, economics and political power grew, and when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awami-league">Awami League</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-mujibur-rahman">Sheikh Mujibur Rahman</a> won the 1970 election but was denied office, the Pakistani army cracked down in March 1971. Independence was declared and, after a war with Indian support, secured in December 1971.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-mujibur-rahman">Sheikh Mujib</a> led the new state until his assassination in 1975; military rulers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziaur-rahman">Ziaur Rahman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussain-muhammad-ershad">Hussain Muhammad Ershad</a> followed. Parliamentary democracy returned in 1991, dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khaleda-zia">Khaleda Zia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-hasina">Sheikh Hasina</a>. Bangladesh has grown through garment exports, remittances and microfinance, pioneered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grameen-bank">Grameen Bank</a>, while facing cyclones and floods.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The republic continues; mass protests in 2024 forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-hasina">Sheikh Hasina</a> from office, and an interim government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-yunus">Muhammad Yunus</a> took charge and organised elections in February 2026. Bangladesh remains one of the most densely populated countries and a leading garment exporter.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bangladesh.jpg" alt="Bangladesh" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bangladesh.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1971 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dhaka</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sheikh Mujibur Rahman, Ziaur Rahman, Sheikh Hasina</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bengali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>People's Republic of Bangladesh, East Pakistan (1955-1971), East Bengal (1947-1955)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1998&amp;focus=bangladesh" aria-label="Bangladesh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7089 1303 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bangladesh lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7409 1559L6864 1211"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6864 1211h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7409 1559h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7409 1559h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dhaka. <a href="/atlas?year=1998&amp;focus=bangladesh">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
